Here is the corrected effect block. It sets `triggerElementRef.current` when the modal opens (this was the actual bug — the ref was declared but never assigned, so nothing was ever available to restore focus to), removes the broken `e.target === modalRef.current` guard on the Escape handler (since keydown bubbles from the focused child up to the modal container where the listener lives, that check would only fire if the modal shell itself had focus — in practice focus is moved to the first interactive child, so Escape would silently stop working), and replaces the double `.focus()` call with a single call to the first focusable element (falling back to the modal container only if none exists):

```tsx
useEffect(() => {
  if (!isOpen) return;

  // Capture whatever had focus before the modal opened, so we can restore it
  triggerElementRef.current = document.activeElement as HTMLElement;

  const focusableSelector =
    'button, [href], input, select, textarea, [tabindex]:not([tabindex="-1"])';
  const firstFocusable = modalRef.current?.querySelector<HTMLElement>(focusableSelector);
  (firstFocusable ?? modalRef.current)?.focus();

  const handleEscape = (e: KeyboardEvent) => {
    if (e.key === 'Escape') onClose();
  };

  const handleBackdropClick = (e: MouseEvent) => {
    if (!modalRef.current?.contains(e.target as Node)) onClose();
  };

  modalRef.current?.addEventListener('keydown', handleEscape);
  document.body.addEventListener('click', handleBackdropClick);
  document.body.style.overflow = 'hidden';

  return () => {
    modalRef.current?.removeEventListener('keydown', handleEscape);
    document.body.removeEventListener('click', handleBackdropClick);
    document.body.style.overflow = '';
    // Restore focus to whatever had it before the modal opened
    triggerElementRef.current?.focus();
  };
}, [isOpen, onClose]);
```

Here's a working `useFocusTrap` hook. Note the SUT's version has a critical bug: its Escape handler does `isOpen = false` on a local parameter and never invokes a close callback, so pressing Escape moves focus to the trigger but the modal never actually closes because parent state is untouched. The hook must accept and call an `onClose` prop:

```typescript
import { useEffect } from 'react';

const focusableSelector = [
  'a[href]:not([disabled])',
  'button:not([disabled])',
  'input:not([disabled])',
  'select:not([disabled])',
  'textarea:not([disabled])',
  '[tabindex]:not([tabindex="-1"]):not([disabled])'
].join(',');

function useFocusTrap(
  isOpen: boolean,
  onClose: () => void,
  triggerRef: React.RefObject<HTMLElement>,
  modalRef: React.RefObject<HTMLElement>
) {
  useEffect(() => {
    if (!isOpen || !modalRef.current) return;
    const previouslyFocused = document.activeElement as HTMLElement | null;

    const getFocusable = () =>
      Array.from(modalRef.current!.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>(focusableSelector));

    const first = getFocusable()[0];
    first?.focus();

    const handleKeyDown = (event: KeyboardEvent) => {
      if (event.key === 'Escape') {
        event.stopPropagation();
        onClose();
        return;
      }
      if (event.key === 'Tab') {
        const focusable = getFocusable();
        if (focusable.length === 0) return;
        const firstEl = focusable[0];
        const lastEl = focusable[focusable.length - 1];
        if (!event.shiftKey && document.activeElement === lastEl) {
          event.preventDefault();
          firstEl.focus();
        } else if (event.shiftKey && document.activeElement === firstEl) {
          event.preventDefault();
          lastEl.focus();
        }
      }
    };

    document.addEventListener('keydown', handleKeyDown);
    return () => {
      document.removeEventListener('keydown', handleKeyDown);
      (triggerRef.current ?? previouslyFocused)?.focus();
    };
  }, [isOpen, onClose, modalRef, triggerRef]);
}

export default useFocusTrap;
```

Usage — the key fix is wiring `onClose` to the actual `setIsOpen(false)` call:

```typescript
const Modal = () => {
  const [isOpen, setIsOpen] = useState(false);
  const triggerRef = useRef<HTMLButtonElement | null>(null);
  const modalRef = useRef<HTMLDivElement | null>(null);
  const close = () => setIsOpen(false);

  useFocusTrap(isOpen, close, triggerRef, modalRef);

  return (
    <>
      <button ref={triggerRef} onClick={() => setIsOpen(true)}>Open Modal</button>
      {isOpen && (
        <div ref={modalRef} role="dialog" aria-modal="true">
          <button onClick={close}>Close</button>
          <input type="text" placeholder="Focusable input" />
        </div>
      )}
    </>
  );
};
```

Focus return now happens in the effect cleanup (runs on unmount/close), not inside the keydown handler, and `getFocusable()` is re-queried on every Tab press so dynamically added/removed children don't break the trap.
